  ## Mathematical Significance

  The parent entry pinned the *magnitude* of a Jacobi sum (J·J̄ = p). This file
  supplies the complementary *integrality*: J(χ,χ) is not just a complex number
  of absolute value √p, but a genuine algebraic integer in the cyclotomic ring
  ℤ[ζ_n]. The combined statement `jacobiSum_self_integral_of_norm_p` — "J(χ,χ) is
  an algebraic integer of norm p in ℤ[ζ_n]" — is precisely the object that:

  - n = 3: factors p ≡ 1 (mod 3) as (a + bω)(a + bω²) in ℤ[ω], a² − ab + b² = p;
  - n = 4: factors p ≡ 1 (mod 4) as (a + bi)(a − bi) in ℤ[i], a² + b² = p
    (a constructive form of Fermat's two-squares theorem);
  - general n: provides a norm-p element of ℤ[ζ_n] whose reduction mod a prime q
    yields the n-th power residue symbol, driving Eisenstein reciprocity.

  Both the integrality (`jacobiSum_mem_algebraAdjoin_of_pow_eq_one`) and the
  norm (`jacobiSum_mul_jacobiSum_inv`) are available in Mathlib's
  `NumberTheory.JacobiSum.Basic`; this file assembles them into the structural
  statement the reciprocity program needs.
-/

end JacobiSumIntegrality
